{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>A people great and etc. you know, and you have heard.</b> It is difficult: if he knows, what significance is there in the mention of hearing after he knows? Further, why did he say twice <b>you and you</b>, and was it not sufficient to say ״you know and have heard״? Now, the intention is that He is speaking with two groups: a group great in quantity — they are the people of Israel, and a group great in quality — they are Joshua and Caleb, who are the great ones of Israel, and especially Joshua, who is designated to be king over Israel. And He began to speak with the great ones, who are Joshua and Caleb, and said <b>you know</b> — for they knew with clear knowledge through their own feet [i.e., by traversing] the land; and corresponding to the multitudes of Israel He said <b>and you have heard</b> from the mouth of the tellers. And with this the fine points are resolved."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:16:11.404951231Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}